# BEGIN sigavdi.email
# sigavdi.email is the newsletter's own name. It is an alias on this
# application, and it is the link under SIGAVDI in the footer of every campaign.
# It has exactly one destination: https://avdi.codes/sigavdi/.
#
# Installed by script/deploy-sigavdi-redirect. Everything between the BEGIN and
# END markers is replaced wholesale on each install.
#
# mod_rewrite, not WordPress: the rule runs before PHP, so the newsletter's
# public name keeps answering when the blog cannot boot, and no plugin can be
# deactivated out from under it. It is also how this host already forwards its
# course URLs to graceful.dev.
#
# Every path collapses to /sigavdi/ rather than being carried across. Because
# the hostname is an alias on this application, an unredirected
# sigavdi.email/some-article serves that article -- the entire blog, duplicated
# under a name that means one page, each copy a rival canonical URL. Carrying
# the path across would preserve those duplicates instead of retiring them, and
# a person who clicked "SIGAVDI" wants the newsletter, not whatever the path
# happened to be.
#
# QSD for the same reason: /sigavdi/?p=123 and /sigavdi/?s=term are the blog's
# own query vars, and letting them ride along would land the reader somewhere
# other than the newsletter.
#
# /.well-known/ is exempt. Let's Encrypt renews this hostname's certificate by
# fetching a token under that path, and a 301 to a page that answers 200 with
# the wrong body fails validation. The certificate would expire ninety days
# later with nothing to connect the two events.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
RewriteEngine On

RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^(www\.)?sigavdi\.email$ [NC]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/\.well-known/
RewriteRule ^ https://avdi.codes/sigavdi/ [R=301,QSD,L]
</IfModule>
# END sigavdi.email
